items.workedExampleMarkdown:
Ashgrove's rule set, written out in full:

- an administrator may do anything;
- an agent may view and comment on any ticket belonging to their own team;
- an agent may edit a ticket only when it is assigned to them;
- a customer may view and comment on their own tickets, and may never edit;
- nobody except an administrator may change a closed ticket.

Written as scattered checks, that becomes five `if` statements in four files. Written as one policy it becomes one readable function:

```js
function permit(actor, ticket, action) {
  if (actor.role === 'admin') return true;

  // A closed ticket is read-only for everyone else, whatever their role.
  if (ticket.status === 'closed' && action !== 'view') return false;

  if (actor.role === 'agent') {
    if (actor.teamId !== ticket.teamId) return false;
    return action === 'edit' ? ticket.assigneeId === actor.id : true;
  }

  if (actor.role === 'customer') {
    if (ticket.ownerId !== actor.id) return false;
    return action !== 'edit';
  }

  return false;
}
```

Three details carry most of the value.

The **closed-ticket rule is stated once**, above the role branches, because it applies across roles. Pushed down into each branch it would be repeated three times and eventually updated in two of them.

The function **ends in `return false`**. An actor whose role is not recognised - a new role added next quarter, or a malformed record - is denied. A policy that ends in `return true` fails open, and a rule that fails open is worse than no rule, because it reads as if it were protecting something.

It **takes the ticket, not a ticket id**. The caller has already loaded the record, so the policy cannot accidentally authorise against a different row than the one that gets returned. That gap - check one record, return another - is a real class of bug.

items.promptMarkdown:
A pull request simplifies Ashgrove's ticket handler. The stated goal is 'remove duplication'.

```js
// before
const ticket = await tickets.findFirst({
  where: { id: params.id, teamId: actor.teamId },
});
if (!ticket) return reply.status(404).send({ error: notFound });

// after
const ticket = await tickets.findUnique({ where: { id: params.id } });
if (!ticket) return reply.status(404).send({ error: notFound });
if (actor.role !== 'agent') return reply.status(403).send({ error: forbidden });
```

The author notes that every caller of this endpoint is an authenticated agent, and that the tests still pass. What is the most serious problem with the change?

Implement `permitTicket(actor, ticket, action)` for Ashgrove. Return `true` when the action is allowed and `false` otherwise.

`actor` has `id`, `role` (`'admin'`, `'agent'` or `'customer'`) and `teamId`.
`ticket` has `ownerId`, `teamId`, `assigneeId` and `status`.
`action` is `'view'`, `'edit'` or `'comment'`.

The rules, in the order the business states them:

- an administrator may do anything;
- otherwise, a ticket whose `status` is `'closed'` allows only `'view'`;
- an agent may act on tickets belonging to their own team, but may `'edit'` only a ticket whose `assigneeId` is their own id;
- a customer may act on tickets they own, and may never `'edit'`;
- anything else is denied.

Do not modify either argument.

solution.code:
function permitTicket(actor, ticket, action) {
  if (actor.role === 'admin') {
    return true;
  }
  if (ticket.status === 'closed' && action !== 'view') {
    return false;
  }
  if (actor.role === 'agent') {
    if (actor.teamId !== ticket.teamId) {
      return false;
    }
    if (action === 'edit') {
      return ticket.assigneeId === actor.id;
    }
    return true;
  }
  if (actor.role === 'customer') {
    if (ticket.ownerId !== actor.id) {
      return false;
    }
    return action !== 'edit';
  }
  return false;
}

grading.starterCode:
function permitTicket(actor, ticket, action) {
  // Roles alone are not enough: the ticket and the action both matter.
  return actor.role === 'admin';
}

items.workedExampleMarkdown:
Two handlers from the same Ashgrove file. One validates carefully and is still wrong.

```js
// Input: validated shape, unbounded magnitude.
const query = listSchema.parse(request.query);   // { limit: number }
const rows = await tickets.findMany({ take: query.limit });
```

`limit` is certainly a number. `limit=2000000` is also a number, and this endpoint will now attempt to load two million rows, serialise them, and hold the result in memory. The schema answered 'is it the right type?' and nobody asked 'is it a reasonable size?'. The fix is a bound in the schema itself - `z.coerce.number().int().min(1).max(100).default(20)` - so the constraint lives with the definition rather than in whichever handler remembers it.

```js
// Output: no boundary at all.
const user = await users.findUnique({ where: { id } });
return reply.send(user);
```

Today that returns the columns that exist today. The bug is scheduled rather than present: the moment somebody adds `passwordResetToken` or `internalRiskNote` to the table, this endpoint publishes it, and the change that broke it is a migration in a different pull request that touched no handler.

```js
// Output: an explicit allow-list.
function publicUser(user) {
  return { id: user.id, displayName: user.displayName, createdAt: user.createdAt };
}
```

Now a new column is private until someone deliberately adds it here. The reviewer of that migration does not have to remember which endpoints return this table - the default carried the decision for them.

Note what makes this work: the allow-list is *positive*. `delete user.passwordHash` before sending is the deny-list version, and it protects exactly the one column somebody thought of.

items.promptMarkdown:
Ashgrove returns ticket data to three audiences. Implement `publicTicket(ticket, audience)`.

`audience` is `'customer'`, `'agent'` or `'admin'`.

Return a **new** object containing only the permitted fields, in this order of generosity:

- a customer sees `id`, `subject`, `status` and `createdAt`;
- an agent sees everything a customer sees, plus `assigneeId` and `internalNotes`;
- an administrator sees everything an agent sees, plus `riskScore`.

Two rules that make this an allow-list rather than a filter:

- a field that is permitted but **absent** from `ticket` must be omitted from the result, not included as `undefined`;
- a field present on `ticket` that is not permitted must never appear, **including fields this exercise has never heard of**.

An unrecognised audience gets an empty object. Do not modify the input.

solution.code:
function publicTicket(ticket, audience) {
  const customerFields = ['id', 'subject', 'status', 'createdAt'];
  const agentFields = customerFields.concat(['assigneeId', 'internalNotes']);
  const adminFields = agentFields.concat(['riskScore']);

  let permitted;
  if (audience === 'customer') {
    permitted = customerFields;
  } else if (audience === 'agent') {
    permitted = agentFields;
  } else if (audience === 'admin') {
    permitted = adminFields;
  } else {
    permitted = [];
  }

  const result = {};
  for (const field of permitted) {
    if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(ticket, field)) {
      result[field] = ticket[field];
    }
  }
  return result;
}

grading.starterCode:
function publicTicket(ticket, audience) {
  // Copying the record and removing what you remember is a deny-list.
  const result = Object.assign({}, ticket);
  delete result.riskScore;
  return result;
}

items.promptMarkdown:
This Ashgrove endpoint was reviewed and approved on the strength of its input validation.

```js
const searchSchema = z.object({
  q: z.string().min(1).max(200),
  limit: z.coerce.number().int(),
});

app.get('/tickets/search', async (request, reply) => {
  const { q, limit } = searchSchema.parse(request.query);
  const rows = await tickets.findMany({
    where: { ownerId: request.owner.id, subject: { contains: q } },
    take: limit,
  });
  return reply.send(rows);
});
```

Ownership scoping is correct and the search term is bounded. Which pair of problems would you raise in review?

solution.code:
function parseListQuery(raw) {
  const allowedStatus = ['open', 'pending', 'closed'];
  const allowedSort = ['createdAt', 'status'];

  const parsed = Number.parseInt(raw.limit, 10);
  const limit = Number.isNaN(parsed) ? 20 : Math.min(Math.max(parsed, 1), 100);

  const status = allowedStatus.includes(raw.status) ? raw.status : null;
  const sort = allowedSort.includes(raw.sort) ? raw.sort : 'createdAt';

  return { limit: limit, status: status, sort: sort };
}

grading.starterCode:
function parseListQuery(raw) {
  // Trusts the caller: no bound, no allow-list.
  return {
    limit: Number.parseInt(raw.limit, 10),
    status: raw.status,
    sort: raw.sort,
  };
}

items.workedExampleMarkdown:
The three most common ways a credential reaches a log, and what each needs.

```js
// 1. The reassuring start-up line.
logger.info({ config }, 'configuration loaded');
```

This prints `DATABASE_URL`, which contains the database password, into the log store - where it is retained, indexed, and readable by everyone with log access, which is a much larger group than everyone with production database access. Log the *names* of the settings that loaded, or nothing at all.

```js
// 2. The helpful error handler.
logger.error({ req: request }, 'request failed');
```

A serialised request carries its headers, and the headers carry `authorization` and `cookie`. This is why logging libraries ship redaction configuration and why the request serialiser should be an allow-list of fields - method, path, request id - rather than the whole object.

```js
// 3. The build-time inline.
const key = import.meta.env.VITE_STRIPE_SECRET;
```

Anything the bundler is allowed to inline ends up in a file served to every visitor. The prefix convention exists precisely to make the boundary visible: a server-side secret must never acquire the public prefix, however convenient it would be.

Redaction itself has a subtlety worth internalising. Matching on key names catches `password` and `apiKey`, and misses `dbConnection: 'postgres://user:hunter2@host/db'`, where the secret is inside a value that has an innocuous key. Robust redaction looks at shape as well as name - and even then it is a safety net, not a substitute for not passing the secret to the logger in the first place.

solution.code:
function redactEntry(entry) {
  const nameFragments = ['password', 'secret', 'token', 'authorization'];

  function unsafeName(key) {
    const lower = key.toLowerCase();
    for (const fragment of nameFragments) {
      if (lower.includes(fragment)) {
        return true;
      }
    }
    return false;
  }

  function unsafeShape(value) {
    if (typeof value !== 'string') {
      return false;
    }
    const scheme = value.indexOf('://');
    if (scheme === -1) {
      return false;
    }
    return value.indexOf('@', scheme) !== -1;
  }

  const result = {};
  for (const key of Object.keys(entry)) {
    const value = entry[key];
    result[key] = unsafeName(key) || unsafeShape(value) ? '[redacted]' : value;
  }
  return result;
}

grading.starterCode:
function redactEntry(entry) {
  // Only catches the exact key 'password'.
  const result = Object.assign({}, entry);
  if ('password' in result) {
    result.password = '[redacted]';
  }
  return result;
}
